const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const HL6={title:'Mental health and the brain',hook:'Mental health is health. It shapes how we think, feel, handle stress, relate to others and make choices—and it is closely tied to physical health through sleep, nutrition, activity and the brain itself. Mental health is 10% of the Healthy Living test, and it may also be the focus of your personal goal.',objectives:['Explain the connection between the brain, body and mental health.','Describe stress, the stress response and healthy coping strategies.','Recognize signs of common mental health conditions.','Identify ways to get help and support others.'],sections:[
['The brain and mental health',[
'The brain is an energy-hungry organ: although it is only about **2% of body weight**, it uses roughly **20% of the body’s energy**, relying mainly on **glucose**—a fact reflected in the NASM course’s sample question. Neurotransmitters such as **serotonin**, **dopamine** and **norepinephrine** influence mood, motivation and attention.',
'Physical habits affect the brain: **sleep** consolidates memory and regulates emotions, **physical activity** releases mood-boosting chemicals and supports brain health and **balanced nutrition** provides steady energy. Substance use can disrupt brain chemistry, especially during adolescence, when the brain is still developing.',
"Omega-3 fatty acids, found in fish, walnuts and flaxseed, are important components of brain cell membranes and are studied for their role in mood and cognitive health.",
"Regular physical activity has been shown to reduce symptoms of anxiety and depression for many people, making exercise both a physical and mental health strategy.",
match('Brain fact and meaning',[['About 20% of energy use','The brain’s share of the body’s energy'],['Glucose','The brain’s main fuel'],['Serotonin','Neurotransmitter linked to mood'],['Dopamine','Neurotransmitter linked to motivation and reward'],['Sleep','Supports memory and emotion regulation'],['Exercise','Releases mood-boosting chemicals']])]],
['Stress and the stress response',[
'**Stress** is the body’s response to demands or threats. The **stress response** (fight-or-flight) releases **adrenaline** and **cortisol**, raising heart rate, breathing and blood sugar. Short-term stress can help performance, but **chronic stress** can harm sleep, immunity, digestion, mood and heart health.',
'Common teen stressors include school, relationships, activities, family and social media. Recognizing your personal warning signs—headaches, irritability, trouble sleeping—helps you respond early.',
"Social media can contribute to stress through comparison, cyberbullying and lost sleep. Setting time limits and turning off notifications at night are practical ways to reduce its impact.",
sort('Short-term helpful or chronic harmful?',['Short-term, can help','Chronic, can harm'],[['Extra focus before a big game',0],['Months of poor sleep from worry',1],['Quick energy to meet a deadline',0],['Frequent stress headaches every day',1],['Ongoing irritability and stomach problems',1]])]],
['Healthy coping strategies',[
'Healthy coping strategies include **physical activity**, **deep breathing**, **mindfulness and meditation**, **adequate sleep**, **time management**, **hobbies**, **time outdoors**, **journaling**, **limiting screen time** and **talking with trusted people**. Unhealthy coping—substance use, avoidance, self-harm or extreme eating or exercise—worsens problems.',
'The guidelines’ sample goal uses **10 minutes of daily meditation** to reduce stress headaches, tracked in a pain diary—an example of a measurable mental health goal.',
"Healthy coping takes practice. Trying a strategy for a few weeks and tracking how you feel shows which methods work best for you.",
"Mindfulness means paying attention to the present moment without judgment. Even short daily practice can lower stress and improve focus for many people.",
ex('Box breathing','Practice a simple breathing technique.',['Breathe in slowly for 4 counts','Hold for 4 counts','Breathe out slowly for 4 counts','Hold for 4 counts and repeat for 1–2 minutes'],'A quick, evidence-informed way to calm the stress response.')]],
['Common mental health conditions',[
'**Anxiety disorders** involve excessive worry or fear that interferes with daily life. **Depression** involves persistent sadness or loss of interest, changes in sleep or appetite, low energy and difficulty concentrating, lasting two weeks or more. **Eating disorders** involve serious disturbances in eating behaviors and body image. **ADHD** affects attention and impulse control.',
'Mental health conditions are **common** and **treatable**—through therapy, lifestyle changes and sometimes medication. Stigma can prevent people from seeking help, so talking openly and respectfully matters.',
"Early warning signs that someone may need help include withdrawing from friends, changes in sleep or appetite, declining grades, irritability and loss of interest in activities they used to enjoy.",
match('Condition and key feature',[['Depression','Persistent sadness or loss of interest for 2+ weeks'],['Generalized anxiety','Excessive, ongoing worry'],['Panic disorder','Sudden episodes of intense fear'],['Eating disorders','Disturbed eating and body image'],['ADHD','Difficulty with attention and impulse control'],['Insomnia','Persistent difficulty sleeping']])]],
['Getting help and helping others',[
'If you or someone you know is struggling, talk to a **trusted adult**—a parent, school counselor, teacher, coach or health care provider. In a crisis, call or text **988** (the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or call **911** for immediate danger.',
'To support a friend: **listen without judgment**, express care, encourage them to talk to a trusted adult or professional and do not keep serious safety concerns secret. Take any mention of self-harm seriously.',
"School counselors, psychologists and social workers are trained to help students with stress, anxiety and depression, and many schools offer confidential support.",
"Seeking help is a sign of strength, not weakness. Encouraging open conversations about mental health in families and schools reduces stigma and helps people get support sooner.",
order('Helping a struggling friend',['Notice warning signs','Ask how they are doing and listen','Show care without judgment','Encourage talking to a trusted adult','Share crisis resources such as 988','Tell an adult if safety is at risk'])]],
['Mental health as a SMART goal',[
'Mental health goals must still be **measurable**. Examples: practicing mindfulness 10 minutes daily and tracking stress ratings; reducing nightly screen time to improve sleep and logging sleep hours; or journaling three times a week and recording mood. Choose measures you can track honestly.',
'Discuss mental health goals with a licensed provider, especially if you have symptoms of a condition. The goal should support, not replace, professional care.',
"Combining a mental health goal with physical habits, such as exercise or sleep, can be effective, since both affect mood. Track both the behavior and how you feel.",
sort('Measurable mental health goal?',['Measurable','Not measurable'],[['Meditate 10 minutes daily and rate stress 1–10 each evening',0],['“Feel happier”',1],['Sleep 8 hours on school nights, tracked in a log',0],['“Worry less someday”',1],['Journal 3 times a week and log mood ratings',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Mental health check',{a:{text:'A friend says they have felt hopeless for weeks and mentions not wanting to be around anymore. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Take it seriously, stay with them, share 988 and tell a trusted adult immediately','b','Correct.',true],['Promise to keep it secret','b','Safety concerns must be shared with an adult.',false]]},b:{text:'You want a mental health SMART goal. Which is best?',choices:[['Practice 10 minutes of mindfulness daily and track stress ratings for 12 weeks','c','Correct.',true],['“Be less stressed”','c','Make it measurable and time-bound.',false]]},c:{text:'You respond safely and set a measurable goal.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The brain uses about 20% of the body’s energy (mainly glucose); neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine affect mood; sleep, activity and nutrition support mental health.','Stress triggers adrenaline and cortisol; chronic stress harms health; healthy coping includes activity, breathing, mindfulness, sleep, time management and talking with trusted people.','Anxiety, depression, eating disorders and ADHD are common and treatable; seek help from trusted adults and use 988 in a crisis; mental health SMART goals need honest, measurable tracking and provider guidance.'],terms:[['Stress response','The body’s fight-or-flight reaction involving adrenaline and cortisol.'],['Neurotransmitter','A chemical messenger between nerve cells.'],['Coping strategy','An action used to manage stress or emotions.']],mcqs:[q('About what percentage of the body’s glucose and energy does the brain use?','About 20%','About 2%','About 50%','About 80%','Sample item.'),q('Which hormone is released during the stress response?','Cortisol','Insulin','Melatonin','Estrogen only','Stress.'),q('Which is a healthy coping strategy?','Deep breathing','Substance use','Avoiding all people','Skipping sleep','Coping.'),q('Depression symptoms typically last at least:','Two weeks','One hour','One day','Ten years','Definition.'),q('Which number reaches the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline?','988','411','311','211','Resource.'),q('If a friend mentions self-harm, you should:','Tell a trusted adult right away','Keep it secret','Ignore it','Post about it','Safety.'),q('Which is a measurable mental health goal?','Meditate 10 minutes daily and rate stress nightly','Feel better','Be calmer someday','Worry less','SMART.'),q('Mental health conditions are:','Common and treatable','Rare and untreatable','Always permanent','Signs of weakness','Facts.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how the brain, stress and healthy habits affect mental health, and how to get help.',rubric:[['Brain',['20%','glucose','neurotransmitters','serotonin','sleep','exercise']],['Stress',['stress response','adrenaline','cortisol','chronic','warning signs']],['Coping and conditions',['breathing','mindfulness','activity','anxiety','depression','treatable','stigma']],['Help',['trusted adult','counselor','988','911','listen','tell']]],model:'Mental health is closely linked to the brain and body. The brain makes up only about 2% of body weight but uses roughly 20% of the body’s energy, relying mainly on glucose, and neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine influence mood and motivation. Sleep helps consolidate memories and regulate emotions, physical activity releases mood-boosting chemicals and balanced nutrition provides steady energy. When we face demands, the stress response releases adrenaline and cortisol, raising heart rate and blood sugar; short-term stress can improve focus, but chronic stress can disrupt sleep, weaken immunity and harm mood and heart health. Healthy coping strategies include exercise, box breathing, mindfulness, enough sleep, time management, hobbies, time outdoors, journaling, limiting screens and talking with trusted people, while substance use and avoidance make problems worse. Common conditions such as anxiety disorders, depression—persistent sadness or loss of interest for two weeks or more—eating disorders and ADHD are common and treatable, and reducing stigma helps people seek care. If I or a friend were struggling, I would talk to a trusted adult such as a parent or school counselor, and in a crisis call or text 988 or call 911 for immediate danger. To support a friend, I would listen without judgment, encourage professional help and never keep safety concerns secret. A mental health SMART goal could be meditating 10 minutes daily for 12 weeks and rating stress each night, discussed with a licensed provider.'},sources:[RULES,NIMH,LIFELINE]};

export const HL7={title:'Wellness through the lifespan',hook:'Health needs change from infancy to older adulthood, and wellness involves far more than the absence of disease. Wellness through the lifespan is 20% of the Healthy Living test. Understanding the dimensions of wellness and how health needs shift at each life stage helps you support patients—and yourself—at any age.',objectives:['Describe the dimensions of wellness.','Explain health needs at each life stage.','Identify key milestones and screenings across the lifespan.','Apply wellness concepts to a personal goal.'],sections:[
['Dimensions of wellness',[
'**Wellness** is an active process of making choices toward a healthy, fulfilling life. Many models describe multiple dimensions: **physical** (activity, nutrition, sleep), **emotional** (coping, self-awareness), **social** (relationships, belonging), **intellectual** (learning, creativity), **spiritual** (purpose, values), **occupational** (satisfying work or school), **environmental** (safe, healthy surroundings) and **financial** (managing resources).',
'The dimensions interact: poor sleep affects mood, financial stress affects health and social connection supports emotional wellness. Your Healthy Living goal may come from any dimension.',
"Some models list six or seven dimensions; the exact number varies, but the idea is the same—wellness is multidimensional and balanced across many areas of life.",
match('Dimension and example',[['Physical','Getting regular exercise'],['Emotional','Managing stress in healthy ways'],['Social','Building supportive friendships'],['Intellectual','Learning a new skill'],['Environmental','Reducing exposure to secondhand smoke'],['Financial','Creating a budget']])]],
['Infancy and childhood',[
'**Infancy (0–1)** is a period of rapid growth. Key wellness needs include **breastfeeding or formula**, **immunizations**, **safe sleep** (placing babies on their backs), **developmental screenings** and responsive caregiving. **Early childhood (1–5)** involves language, motor and social development, nutritious foods, active play and safety (car seats, poison prevention).',
'**School-age children (6–12)** need daily physical activity, balanced nutrition, enough sleep (about 9–12 hours), dental care, vaccines and positive relationships. Habits formed early often last a lifetime.',
"Well-child visits track growth and development and provide vaccines on a recommended schedule, catching concerns early when they are easiest to address.",
"Childhood obesity has become a significant public health concern, making healthy school meals, daily activity and limited sugary drinks important prevention strategies for young children.",
sort('Infancy or school-age focus?',['Infancy','School-age childhood'],[['Placing a baby on its back to sleep',0],['60 minutes of daily activity at recess and sports',1],['Rapid growth and first vaccines',0],['Learning to read and building friendships',1],['Breastfeeding or formula',0]])]],
['Adolescence and young adulthood',[
'**Adolescence (about 12–18)** brings **puberty**, rapid brain development (the prefrontal cortex, which supports judgment, matures into the mid-20s), identity formation and increasing independence. Key wellness needs include **8–10 hours of sleep**, daily activity, nutrition for growth, mental health support and avoiding tobacco, vaping, alcohol and other drugs.',
'**Young adults (about 18–30s)** build careers, relationships and independent health habits—choosing a primary care provider, managing insurance and continuing preventive care. Habits in these years strongly influence later health.',
"Adolescents are more likely to take risks partly because brain systems for reward develop earlier than those for impulse control. Supportive adults and clear information help teens make safer choices.",
chk('Which brain region, important for judgment, continues maturing into the mid-20s?',['Prefrontal cortex','Brainstem','Cerebellum','Spinal cord'],'Adolescent brain development.')]],
['Middle and older adulthood',[
'**Middle adulthood (about 40–65)** is a time when risks of **heart disease, type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure and some cancers** rise. Screenings such as blood pressure, cholesterol, diabetes and recommended cancer screenings become important, along with stress management and staying active.',
'**Older adulthood (65+)** brings changes in vision, hearing, bone density, muscle mass and sometimes memory. Wellness strategies include **balance and strength training** to prevent falls, social connection, mental stimulation, managing chronic conditions, vaccines (such as flu, pneumococcal and shingles) and planning for future care.',
"Many chronic diseases that appear in middle age begin with habits formed earlier. Prevention at every stage is more effective than treatment later.",
"Women experience menopause in middle adulthood, which can affect bone density and heart health, making weight-bearing exercise and calcium and vitamin D intake especially important.",
match('Life stage and wellness focus',[['Infancy','Safe sleep and immunizations'],['Early childhood','Active play and safety'],['Adolescence','Sleep, mental health and avoiding substances'],['Young adulthood','Building independent health habits'],['Middle adulthood','Screenings for chronic disease'],['Older adulthood','Fall prevention and social connection']])]],
['Growth and development theories',[
'Theories help explain lifespan development. **Erik Erikson** described eight psychosocial stages, each with a central challenge—for example, **identity versus role confusion** in adolescence and **integrity versus despair** in older adulthood. **Jean Piaget** described cognitive stages from sensorimotor (infancy) to formal operational (adolescence), when abstract thinking develops. **Maslow’s hierarchy** places physiological and safety needs before belonging, esteem and self-actualization.',
'These frameworks help health professionals communicate appropriately and support patients at different ages.',
"Health professionals apply these theories by adjusting how they explain care—using simple, concrete language with young children and involving adolescents in decisions about their own health.",
"Developmental milestones—such as walking around 12 months or speaking in short sentences by about age 2—help providers identify children who may need early support.",
order('Maslow’s hierarchy (base to top)',['Physiological needs','Safety','Love and belonging','Esteem','Self-actualization'])]],
['Wellness across your own lifespan',[
'Your choices now shape your future health. Building activity, nutrition, sleep and stress-management habits as a teen lowers risk of chronic disease later. Healthy People 2030 sets national objectives for health across all life stages, emphasizing prevention, equity and well-being.',
'When writing your SMART goal, consider which habits will benefit you now and in the future.',
"Healthy aging includes staying engaged in meaningful activities, maintaining friendships and continuing to learn, all of which support cognitive and emotional health in later life.",
ex('Linking a goal to the lifespan','Connect a teen goal to lifelong wellness (example).',['Goal: strength training 3 days a week for 16 weeks','Now: improves strength, confidence and sports performance','Young adulthood: builds a lifelong exercise habit','Older adulthood: higher bone density and muscle mass may reduce fall and fracture risk'],'A current goal supports wellness across the lifespan.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Lifespan drill',{a:{text:'A grandparent has fallen twice this year. Which wellness strategy is most relevant? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Balance and strength training and a home safety check','b','Correct.',true],['More screen time','b','Fall prevention focuses on balance, strength and safety.',false]]},b:{text:'Which Erikson stage describes the central challenge of adolescence?',choices:[['Identity versus role confusion','c','Correct.',true],['Trust versus mistrust','c','That is infancy.',false]]},c:{text:'You add both to your lifespan study sheet.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Wellness has many interacting dimensions—physical, emotional, social, intellectual, spiritual, occupational, environmental and financial; a Healthy Living goal can come from any dimension.','Health needs change: infants need safe sleep and immunizations; children need activity, nutrition and sleep; adolescents need 8–10 hours of sleep, mental health support and avoiding substances (the prefrontal cortex matures into the mid-20s); adults need screenings; older adults need fall prevention, vaccines and social connection.','Erikson (psychosocial stages), Piaget (cognitive stages) and Maslow (hierarchy of needs) explain development; habits built now shape lifelong health.'],terms:[['Wellness','An active process of making choices toward a healthy, fulfilling life.'],['Prefrontal cortex','The brain region involved in judgment and planning.'],['Maslow’s hierarchy','A model ranking human needs from physiological to self-actualization.']],mcqs:[q('Which dimension of wellness involves relationships?','Social','Physical','Financial','Environmental','Dimensions.'),q('How should infants be placed to sleep?','On their backs','On their stomachs','On their sides','Sitting up','Safe sleep.'),q('About how much sleep do teens need?','8–10 hours','4–5 hours','12–14 hours','6 hours','Adolescence.'),q('Which region matures into the mid-20s?','Prefrontal cortex','Brainstem','Cerebellum','Medulla','Development.'),q('Which Erikson stage occurs in adolescence?','Identity versus role confusion','Trust versus mistrust','Integrity versus despair','Industry versus inferiority','Erikson.'),q('What is at the base of Maslow’s hierarchy?','Physiological needs','Self-actualization','Esteem','Belonging','Maslow.'),q('Which is a key wellness focus for older adults?','Fall prevention','Learning to crawl','Puberty','Teething','Lifespan.'),q('Screenings for heart disease and diabetes become especially important in:','Middle adulthood','Infancy','Early childhood','School age only','Lifespan.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the dimensions of wellness and how health needs change across the lifespan.',rubric:[['Dimensions',['physical','emotional','social','intellectual','environmental','financial','interact']],['Early life',['infancy','safe sleep','immunizations','childhood','activity','sleep']],['Adolescence and adulthood',['adolescence','prefrontal','substances','screenings','chronic disease']],['Older adults and theories',['fall prevention','vaccines','social','erikson','piaget','maslow']]],model:'Wellness is an active process of making choices toward a healthy, fulfilling life, and it includes physical, emotional, social, intellectual, spiritual, occupational, environmental and financial dimensions that affect one another—poor sleep can affect mood, and social connection supports emotional wellness. Health needs change throughout life. Infants need breastfeeding or formula, immunizations, developmental screenings and safe sleep on their backs; young children need nutritious food, active play and safety measures; and school-age children need daily activity, nine to twelve hours of sleep, dental care and supportive relationships. Adolescents experience puberty, identity formation and brain development—the prefrontal cortex, which supports judgment, matures into the mid-20s—so they need eight to ten hours of sleep, nutrition for growth, mental health support and avoidance of tobacco, vaping, alcohol and drugs. Young adults build independent health habits, while middle-aged adults face rising risks of heart disease, diabetes and some cancers and need screenings and stress management. Older adults benefit from balance and strength training to prevent falls, vaccines, social connection, mental stimulation and management of chronic conditions. Theories help explain these changes: Erikson described psychosocial stages such as identity versus role confusion in adolescence, Piaget described cognitive stages ending in abstract thinking, and Maslow’s hierarchy places physiological and safety needs before belonging, esteem and self-actualization. My teen habits shape my health across my lifespan.'},sources:[RULES,LIFE,HP]};

export const HL8={title:'Disease prevention',hook:'Many of the leading causes of death and disability—heart disease, cancer, stroke, type 2 diabetes—are strongly influenced by everyday behaviors. Disease prevention is 20% of the Healthy Living test. This lesson covers chronic and infectious disease prevention, the levels of prevention, screenings and vaccines, and the behaviors that protect health.',objectives:['Distinguish chronic and infectious diseases and their risk factors.','Describe primary, secondary and tertiary prevention.','Explain the role of vaccines, screenings and hygiene.','Identify behaviors that reduce disease risk.'],sections:[
['Chronic disease',[
'**Chronic diseases** last a year or more and require ongoing care or limit daily activities. The leading chronic diseases in the United States include **heart disease, cancer, chronic lung disease, stroke, Alzheimer’s disease, type 2 diabetes** and **chronic kidney disease**. Many share key **modifiable risk factors**: **tobacco use**, **poor nutrition**, **physical inactivity** and **excessive alcohol use**.',
'**Non-modifiable** risk factors include age, genetics and family history. Even with non-modifiable risks, healthy behaviors can lower overall risk.',
"Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States, and cancer is second, according to CDC data. Both are strongly influenced by modifiable behaviors.",
sort('Modifiable or non-modifiable risk factor?',['Modifiable','Non-modifiable'],[['Tobacco use',0],['Age',1],['Physical inactivity',0],['Family history',1],['Poor nutrition',0],['Genetics',1],['Excessive alcohol use',0]])]],
['Cardiovascular disease and diabetes',[
'**Heart disease** often results from **atherosclerosis**—plaque buildup in arteries. Risk factors include **high blood pressure** (hypertension), **high LDL cholesterol**, smoking, diabetes, obesity, inactivity and unhealthy diet. Normal adult blood pressure is generally **below 120/80 mm Hg**.',
'**Type 2 diabetes** occurs when the body does not use insulin effectively, causing high blood sugar. Risk factors include excess weight, inactivity and family history; **prediabetes** can often be reversed with lifestyle changes. **Type 1 diabetes** is an autoimmune condition and is not caused by lifestyle.',
"Blood pressure readings at or above 130/80 mm Hg are generally classified as high blood pressure under current U.S. guidelines, and many people have it without symptoms.",
"Lifestyle changes such as losing a modest amount of weight and increasing physical activity have been shown to significantly reduce the risk of progressing from prediabetes to type 2 diabetes.",
match('Term and meaning',[['Atherosclerosis','Plaque buildup in arteries'],['Hypertension','High blood pressure'],['LDL cholesterol','“Bad” cholesterol that can build plaque'],['HDL cholesterol','“Good” cholesterol that helps remove LDL'],['Type 2 diabetes','Insulin not used effectively'],['Prediabetes','Elevated blood sugar that can often be reversed']])]],
['Cancer prevention',[
'Cancer risk can be reduced by **avoiding tobacco** (the leading preventable cause of cancer death), **limiting alcohol**, **protecting skin** from UV radiation (sunscreen, shade, avoiding tanning beds), **eating plenty of fruits, vegetables and fiber**, staying active, maintaining a healthy weight and getting vaccinated against **HPV** and **hepatitis B**, which can prevent certain cancers.',
'**Screenings** such as those for cervical, breast and colorectal cancer detect cancer early or prevent it by finding precancerous changes, following recommended ages and schedules.',
"Skin cancer is among the most common cancers, and protecting skin from UV radiation with sunscreen of SPF 30 or higher, protective clothing and shade greatly reduces risk.",
chk('Which vaccine helps prevent several cancers?',['HPV vaccine','Tetanus vaccine','Chickenpox vaccine','Flu vaccine'],'HPV causes several cancers.')]],
['Levels of prevention',[
'**Primary prevention** stops disease before it starts: vaccines, healthy eating, exercise, seat belts, sunscreen and not smoking. **Secondary prevention** detects disease early to limit harm: screenings such as blood pressure checks, mammograms and blood sugar tests. **Tertiary prevention** reduces complications of existing disease: cardiac rehabilitation, diabetes management and physical therapy after a stroke.',
'Some models also describe **quaternary prevention**, protecting patients from unnecessary medical interventions.',
"Recommended screening ages and schedules change as research evolves, so health professionals follow current guidance from organizations such as the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force.",
sort('Level of prevention',['Primary','Secondary','Tertiary'],[['Getting a flu vaccine',0],['A blood pressure screening',1],['Cardiac rehabilitation after a heart attack',2],['Wearing sunscreen',0],['A colonoscopy screening',1],['Managing blood sugar to prevent diabetes complications',2]])]],
['Infectious disease prevention',[
'**Infectious diseases** are caused by pathogens—bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites—and spread through contact, droplets, air, food and water or vectors such as mosquitoes. Prevention includes **hand hygiene** (washing with soap for at least **20 seconds**), **vaccination**, **covering coughs and sneezes**, **staying home when sick**, **safe food handling** and **safe sex practices**.',
'Vaccines train the immune system to recognize pathogens and also protect communities through **herd (community) immunity**. Antibiotics treat bacterial infections, not viruses; overuse contributes to **antibiotic resistance**.',
"Vaccines go through extensive testing before approval and continued safety monitoring afterward. Following recommended schedules protects individuals and vulnerable community members.",
"Food safety prevents many infections: clean hands and surfaces, separate raw meat from other foods, cook to safe temperatures and refrigerate leftovers promptly.",
order('Handwashing steps',['Wet hands with clean water','Apply soap','Lather and scrub all surfaces for at least 20 seconds','Rinse well','Dry with a clean towel or air dryer'])]],
['Behaviors that protect health',[
'Key protective behaviors: **avoid tobacco and vaping**, **limit alcohol** and avoid drugs, **eat a healthy diet**, **be physically active**, **sleep enough**, **manage stress**, **maintain a healthy weight**, **use sunscreen**, **wear seat belts and helmets**, **get recommended vaccines and screenings** and **see health care providers regularly**.',
'Small changes add up. A SMART goal in disease prevention might target reducing sugary drinks, increasing activity or improving sleep, measured over time.',
"Vaping and smoking expose users to nicotine, which is highly addictive and harmful to the developing adolescent brain. Avoiding tobacco products is one of the most important prevention behaviors.",
ex('A disease prevention SMART goal','Write a goal related to disease prevention (example).',['Specific: replace sugary drinks with water','Measurable: from 2 sodas a day to none, tracked daily','Attainable: carry a water bottle and keep flavored water at home','Realistic: discussed with a health provider','Timely: by March 1'],'A clear goal that lowers added sugar intake and type 2 diabetes risk factors.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Prevention drill',{a:{text:'A mammogram that finds early breast cancer is which level of prevention? (Practice item.)',choices:[['Secondary prevention','b','Correct.',true],['Primary prevention','b','Screening detects existing disease early.',false]]},b:{text:'A friend wants antibiotics for a cold. What should you explain?',choices:[['Colds are viral; antibiotics do not work on viruses and overuse causes resistance','c','Correct.',true],['Antibiotics cure all infections','c','They treat bacterial infections only.',false]]},c:{text:'You add both to your prevention study notes.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Leading chronic diseases (heart disease, cancer, chronic lung disease, stroke, diabetes) share modifiable risks: tobacco, poor nutrition, inactivity and excessive alcohol; atherosclerosis and hypertension drive heart disease; type 2 diabetes involves insulin resistance and prediabetes is often reversible.','Cancer prevention: avoid tobacco, limit alcohol, protect skin, eat fiber-rich foods, stay active and get HPV and hepatitis B vaccines and recommended screenings.','Primary prevention stops disease (vaccines, healthy habits), secondary detects it early (screenings) and tertiary limits complications (rehabilitation, disease management); prevent infection with handwashing (20+ seconds), vaccines and hygiene; antibiotics do not treat viruses.'],terms:[['Chronic disease','A condition lasting a year or more requiring ongoing care.'],['Primary prevention','Preventing disease before it occurs.'],['Herd immunity','Community protection when many people are immune.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a modifiable risk factor for chronic disease?','Tobacco use','Age','Genetics','Family history','Risk.'),q('Atherosclerosis is:','Plaque buildup in arteries','A viral infection','A bone disease','A skin condition','Definition.'),q('Normal adult blood pressure is generally below:','120/80 mm Hg','160/100 mm Hg','90/40 mm Hg only','200/120 mm Hg','Value.'),q('A flu vaccine is which level of prevention?','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','None','Level.'),q('Cardiac rehabilitation is which level of prevention?','Tertiary','Primary','Secondary','None','Level.'),q('How long should you scrub hands with soap?','At least 20 seconds','5 seconds','2 minutes minimum','1 second','Hygiene.'),q('Antibiotics treat:','Bacterial infections','Viral infections such as colds','All infections','Allergies','Use.'),q('Which vaccine helps prevent certain cancers?','HPV','Tetanus','Measles','Chickenpox','Cancer prevention.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the major risk factors for chronic disease, the levels of prevention and key ways to prevent infectious disease.',rubric:[['Chronic disease',['heart disease','cancer','diabetes','tobacco','nutrition','inactivity','alcohol']],['Specific conditions',['atherosclerosis','blood pressure','cholesterol','insulin','prediabetes','hpv']],['Levels',['primary','secondary','tertiary','vaccines','screenings','rehabilitation']],['Infectious disease',['handwashing','20 seconds','vaccination','herd immunity','antibiotics','resistance']]],model:'Many leading chronic diseases—heart disease, cancer, chronic lung disease, stroke and type 2 diabetes—share four key modifiable risk factors: tobacco use, poor nutrition, physical inactivity and excessive alcohol use, while age, genetics and family history cannot be changed. Heart disease often results from atherosclerosis, the buildup of plaque in arteries, driven by high blood pressure above about 120/80, high LDL cholesterol, smoking and diabetes. Type 2 diabetes occurs when the body does not use insulin effectively, and prediabetes can often be reversed with healthy eating, activity and weight management. Cancer risk can be lowered by avoiding tobacco, limiting alcohol, protecting skin from UV rays, eating fruits, vegetables and fiber, staying active and getting HPV and hepatitis B vaccines. Prevention works at three levels: primary prevention stops disease before it starts through vaccines, healthy habits, seat belts and sunscreen; secondary prevention detects disease early through screenings such as blood pressure checks and mammograms; and tertiary prevention reduces complications, as in cardiac rehabilitation or diabetes management. Infectious diseases are prevented by washing hands with soap for at least 20 seconds, getting vaccinated—which also creates herd immunity—covering coughs, staying home when sick and handling food safely. Antibiotics treat only bacterial infections, not viruses like colds, and overuse contributes to antibiotic resistance. A personal SMART goal, such as replacing two daily sodas with water by March 1, can lower disease risk factors.'},sources:[RULES,CHRONIC,HP]};

export const HL9={title:'The SMART goal, baseline and portfolio',hook:'Your portfolio is the evidence of your healthy living journey. Judges score whether your goal follows the SMART formula, whether it is challenging but attainable, whether your evidence shows measurable progress from a clear baseline and whether the required pages are included. Planning the goal and documentation carefully from the start makes your presentation convincing.',objectives:['Write a SMART goal that is challenging but attainable.','Complete a baseline health assessment relevant to the goal.','Document the journey with measurable evidence.','Assemble a portfolio that meets every requirement.'],sections:[
['Writing a SMART goal',[
'**Specific:** state exactly what you will do and how. **Measurable:** include a number you can track. **Attainable:** within your abilities and resources. **Realistic:** fits your life and is safe. **Timely:** has a deadline within the event period (July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027).',
'The guidelines’ examples show the level of detail expected: increasing water to 64 ounces daily by the end of spring semester using a 20-ounce bottle and a calendar; running a mile in under 8 minutes by June with a fitness log; and reducing stress headaches from daily to twice monthly through 10 minutes of daily meditation tracked in a pain diary.',
"Write the goal in the first person and include the method and tracking tool, as the guideline examples do. A complete goal tells judges what, how, how much and by when.",
sort('SMART element present?',['Specific and measurable','Vague'],[['Walk 8,000 steps daily, tracked by a step counter',0],['Exercise more',1],['Sleep 8 hours on school nights, logged each morning',0],['Eat better',1],['Reduce sugary drinks from 2 a day to 0 by March 1',0]])]],
['Challenging but attainable',[
'Judges score whether the goal is **challenging but attainable**. A goal that is too easy (“drink one glass of water today”) shows little growth; a goal that is too extreme (“lose 30 pounds in a month”) may be unsafe and unrealistic. Aim for meaningful change you can sustain.',
'Consult a **licensed health practitioner** before beginning, especially for goals involving diet, weight or intense exercise. HOSA discourages fad diets and programs not directed by a licensed provider.',
"If your goal turns out to be too easy or too hard early on, discuss it with your advisor. The goal cannot change between competition levels, so get it right at the start.",
chk('Which goal is challenging but attainable for a beginner runner?',['Run a mile in under 9 minutes in 16 weeks, starting from 12 minutes','Run a marathon next week','Walk to the mailbox once','Never exercise again'],'Meaningful and realistic.')]],
['The baseline health assessment',[
'A **baseline health assessment** measures your general health and specific factors relevant to your goal **before** the goal begins. You may use an existing assessment, get one from a licensed provider or create your own. It should include general health information (such as vital signs, activity level, sleep and diet habits) and goal-specific measures (for example, a timed mile, daily water intake, stress ratings or sleep hours).',
'The baseline is both a required portfolio item (5 points) and the starting point for showing progress (10 points for clear, measurable baseline data).',
"Keep a copy of the baseline assessment with the date clearly shown. The date proves the baseline came before the goal started, as the guidelines require.",
match('Goal and baseline measure',[['Improve mile time','Timed mile before training'],['Increase water intake','Average daily ounces for one week'],['Reduce stress headaches','Headache count per week'],['Improve sleep','Average hours per night for two weeks'],['Increase strength','Push-ups completed in one minute'],['Reduce sugary drinks','Drinks per day for one week']])]],
['Documenting the journey',[
'**Evidence of the journey** is any documentation that supports your progress: **logs** (food, water, sleep, exercise), **charts and graphs** of measurements over time, **photos**, **app data**, **letters from professionals** (coach, provider), **journal entries** and **reflections** on challenges and strategies.',
'Record data consistently—same method, same time—so comparisons are fair. Include setbacks and how you responded; honest documentation is more convincing than perfect results.',
"Use the same tool for every measurement—the same scale, app or track—so changes reflect real progress rather than differences in equipment.",
ex('Charting progress','Mile times (invented for practice): Week 1: 12:00; Week 4: 11:15; Week 8: 10:20; Week 12: 9:40; Week 16: 8:55. Summarize.',['Total improvement: 12:00 − 8:55 = 3 minutes 5 seconds','Average improvement: about 46 seconds every 4 weeks','Display as a line graph with dates','Add notes on training changes and setbacks'],'A clear graph shows steady, measurable progress (data invented for practice).')]],
['Medical documentation and privacy',[
'You may include documentation of medical care and progress (such as lab results), at your discretion and with your parent or guardian’s agreement if you are under 18. Include **only what you are comfortable sharing**, and **block out personal information** such as Social Security numbers, insurance numbers and addresses.',
'The **parent/guardian/advisor permission form** is required for competitors 17 or younger. Parents verify that the information is accurate and used with permission; the advisor verifies that the goal is realistic and based on sound research.',
"Discuss privacy decisions with your parent or guardian early, so you know which documents you will include before assembling the portfolio.",
sort('Should be blocked out or excluded?',['Block out or exclude','Fine to include'],[['Social Security number',0],['Home address',0],['Your weekly step counts',1],['Insurance member number',0],['A chart of your sleep hours',1],['A coach’s letter about your progress',1]])]],
['Assembling the portfolio',[
'Required items: **title page** (event name, your name and age, HOSA chapter, division, school, chartered association and specific goal), **baseline health assessment**, **permission form** (if applicable), **evidence of journey** and **reference page(s)** in APA style. Page count, photos and data beyond these are your choice.',
'Organize the evidence chronologically or by measure, use clear headings and tabs and make charts easy to read. The portfolio is a **visual aid** during your presentation and is left with judges afterward for scoring.',
"Use dividers or tabs labeled to match your presentation sections so you can turn quickly to the evidence you are describing.",
order('Portfolio sections',['Title page','Baseline health assessment','Permission form (if 17 or younger)','Evidence of the journey','Reflection and results summary','Reference page(s)'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Portfolio decisions',{a:{text:'Your goal is “get healthier.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you improve it?',choices:[['Make it SMART, such as “Increase daily steps from 4,000 to 8,000 by March 1, tracked by a step counter”','b','Correct.',true],['Keep it broad','b','SMART goals score higher.',false]]},b:{text:'You want to include lab results showing improved blood sugar. What should you do?',choices:[['Include them only with parent agreement and block out personal identifiers','c','Correct.',true],['Include everything, including insurance numbers','c','Protect private information.',false]]},c:{text:'Your goal and portfolio meet every requirement.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Write a SMART goal (specific, measurable, attainable, realistic, timely; July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027) that is challenging but attainable and reviewed with a licensed provider; avoid fad diets.','Complete a baseline health assessment before starting with general and goal-specific measures; document the journey with consistent logs, charts, photos, letters and reflections, including setbacks.','Include title page, baseline assessment, permission form (17 or younger), evidence and APA references; share medical documentation only at your discretion and block out identifiers.'],terms:[['Attainable','Possible to achieve with available abilities and resources.'],['Evidence of journey','Documentation showing progress toward a goal.'],['Permission form','A form signed by a parent/guardian and advisor for competitors 17 or younger.']],mcqs:[q('What does the M in SMART stand for?','Measurable','Meaningful','Medical','Monthly','Formula.'),q('When must the baseline assessment be completed?','Before the goal starts','After the competition','Halfway through','Never','Rule.'),q('Which is a SMART goal?','Increase daily steps from 4,000 to 8,000 by March 1','Exercise more','Be healthier','Eat better','SMART.'),q('Who signs the permission form for competitors under 18?','Parent/guardian and advisor','Only the competitor','The judges','A friend','Rule.'),q('Which information should be blocked out?','Insurance numbers','Step counts','Sleep charts','Coach letters','Privacy.'),q('Which is evidence of the journey?','A weekly exercise log','A blank page','A friend’s opinion','An unrelated photo','Evidence.'),q('HOSA discourages:','Fad diets not directed by a licensed provider','Consulting providers','Tracking progress','Setting goals','Safety.'),q('Which is a required portfolio item?','Reference page(s)','A video','A slideshow','A trophy','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would write your SMART goal, complete a baseline assessment and build your Healthy Living portfolio.',rubric:[['SMART goal',['specific','measurable','attainable','realistic','timely','challenging']],['Baseline',['baseline','before','general health','goal-specific','provider']],['Evidence',['logs','charts','photos','letters','reflection','setbacks','consistent']],['Portfolio and privacy',['title page','permission','references','block out','discretion','visual aid']]],model:'In July, I would complete a baseline health assessment with my doctor that recorded general health—vital signs, activity, sleep and diet habits—and goal-specific measures, including a timed mile of 12 minutes. After discussing options with my doctor, I would write a SMART goal that is challenging but attainable: “I will run one mile in under 9 minutes by May 1 by running after school four days a week, starting with intervals and reducing my time by about 15 seconds every two weeks, tracking each run in a fitness log.” It is specific about what and how, measurable by mile time, attainable and realistic for my schedule with my doctor’s approval and timely within the July 1 to May 15 period. Because I am 16, my parent and advisor would sign the permission form. Throughout the year, I would document my journey consistently, timing my mile on the same track every four weeks, logging runs, sleep and water, graphing my mile times, adding photos and a letter from my coach and writing short reflections on setbacks, such as a two-week break for illness, and how I adjusted. My portfolio would include a title page with the event name, my name and age, chapter, division, school, chartered association and goal; the baseline assessment; the permission form; the evidence organized chronologically with clear headings; a results summary; and an APA reference page. I would include only information I am comfortable sharing and block out any personal identifiers such as insurance numbers or my address.'},sources:[RULES,NASM]};

export const HL10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'In five minutes, you tell the story of your healthy living journey—what you set out to do, where you started, how you worked toward it and what changed—while using your portfolio as evidence. Context and portfolio incorporation alone are worth 40 points. A confident, evidence-rich story shows judges both your knowledge and your commitment.',objectives:['Explain the SMART goal and why you chose it.','Present baseline and progress evidence clearly.','Describe the journey with confidence and reflection.','Use the portfolio effectively as a visual aid.'],sections:[
['Required content',[
'The presentation must include an **explanation of your personal healthy living SMART goal** and **evidence of the journey**, using the portfolio to show, demonstrate and discuss progress. The more substantive your explanations and documentation, the easier it is for judges to evaluate progress.',
'Only the **portfolio** may be shown to judges. Notecards are allowed (electronic notes are not shown). A time card appears with **one minute remaining**. Afterward, you leave the portfolio with judges, who score the title page, assessment, permission form and references.',
"Practice the presentation using the actual portfolio so you know exactly where each page is. Smooth page turns support confident delivery.",
"Organize notecards by the same sections as your portfolio tabs, with one key number or phrase per section, so you stay on track without reading.",
flip('Round Two content scoring',[['SMART formula','10'],['Consistent with healthy living','10'],['Challenging but attainable','10'],['Worked toward or maintained goal','10'],['Healthy proof of progress','10'],['Clear baseline data','10']])]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A clear structure: **Introduction and goal (about 45 seconds)**—state your SMART goal and why you chose it. **Baseline (about 45 seconds)**—show your starting measurements. **Journey (about 2 minutes)**—describe strategies, challenges and adjustments, pointing to logs and charts. **Results (about 1 minute)**—show measurable progress and what it means for your health. **Reflection and close (about 30 seconds)**—what you learned and how you will maintain healthy habits.',
'Rehearse to about **4 minutes 45 seconds**.',
"Start with a brief hook, such as your starting measurement or a moment that motivated your goal, to draw judges into your story immediately.",
"Practice explaining why your goal mattered to you personally. A brief, honest reason—such as wanting more energy for sports or reducing headaches—helps judges connect with your story.",
ex('Timing plan','Plan a five-minute presentation (one possible plan).',['Goal and why: 0:45','Baseline: 0:45','Journey and strategies: 2:00','Results: 1:00','Reflection and close: 0:15','Total: 4:45'],'A balanced plan with a margin.')]],
['Context: telling the journey',[
'**Context** (20 points) rewards a competitor who **speaks confidently** about the journey and is **evidently motivated** by the results. Share specific moments: the first week, a setback and how you overcame it, a turning point and what kept you going. Explain the healthy living knowledge that guided your choices—for example, why progressive overload helped your running or why hydration improved your energy.',
'Speak genuinely; judges can tell when a competitor truly lived the goal.',
"Connect your experience to test knowledge. Mentioning principles like FITT, progressive overload or the Dietary Guidelines shows judges that your choices were informed.",
"If you maintained a goal rather than improving it, explain why maintenance was meaningful and how you overcame challenges to keep the habit going.",
sort('Strong or weak context?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“In week six I got sick, so I restarted at a lower distance and rebuilt over two weeks.”',0],['“I did the goal and it was fine.”',1],['“Tracking water each day showed me I drank least on weekends, so I set phone reminders.”',0],['“I think it worked.”',1],['“My energy in afternoon classes improved once I reached 64 ounces.”',0]])]],
['Incorporating the portfolio',[
'**Incorporation of the portfolio** (20 points) rewards using it actively as evidence. Turn to tabbed sections as you speak, hold up charts so judges can see them, point to specific data points and show before-and-after comparisons. Practice opening the portfolio smoothly to each page.',
'Organize the portfolio to match your presentation order so you are not searching for pages.',
"Use sticky tabs to mark key pages, and practice pointing to the exact data point you mention so judges can follow along.",
"Practice with a friend acting as a judge who asks you to show specific evidence. This rehearsal builds confidence in finding pages quickly.",
order('Using the portfolio in the talk',['Open to the title page and state the goal','Show the baseline assessment','Point to logs during the journey section','Hold up the progress graph for results','Show a coach or provider letter','Close on a reflection page'])]],
['Presenting evidence and results',[
'Present results **clearly and measurably**: “My mile time improved from 12:00 to 8:55—a 3-minute, 5-second improvement in 16 weeks.” Explain **healthy proof of progress**—how the change was achieved safely and what health benefits followed, such as better sleep, energy or blood pressure.',
'If you did not fully reach the goal, explain progress made, what you learned and your plan to continue. Judges score working toward or maintaining the goal, not perfection.',
"Explain what the numbers mean for your health. A lower resting heart rate, for example, often reflects improved cardiovascular fitness.",
"Mention any unexpected benefits you noticed, such as better sleep or mood, and explain how they relate to your goal. These show a broader understanding of healthy living.",
chk('Which results statement is strongest?',['“My daily water intake rose from 24 to 66 ounces, shown in my 20-week log.”','“I drank more water.”','“It went pretty well.”','“I mostly did it.”'],'Specific and measurable.')]],
['Delivery and professionalism',[
'Delivery—**voice**, **stage presence** and **diction**—is worth 5 points each. Speak clearly and with energy, make eye contact, stand confidently while holding the portfolio and avoid fillers. Use correct terms from the NASM course and your health knowledge.',
'Dress professionally for bonus points. Bring the printed portfolio in good condition, since judges will review it after you leave.',
"End with how you will maintain your new habits after the competition. Judges value goals that lead to lasting healthy living, not just a temporary change.",
sort('Helps or hurts delivery?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Holding charts toward judges',0],['Reading notecards the whole time',1],['Eye contact while describing setbacks',0],['Flipping pages randomly',1],['Confident, clear voice',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation day',{a:{text:'In practice, you spend three minutes on your baseline assessment. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Shorten the baseline and spend more time on the journey and results','b','Correct.',true],['Keep it—baseline is everything','b','Context and evidence carry the most points.',false]]},b:{text:'You did not fully reach your goal. How do you present it?',choices:[['Show measurable progress, explain challenges and describe your plan to continue','c','Correct.',true],['Pretend you reached it','c','Honesty and progress are what judges score.',false]]},c:{text:'Your presentation is honest, evidence-based and confident.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Present your SMART goal and evidence of the journey within five minutes, showing only the portfolio (left with judges afterward); a card shows one minute remaining.','Structure about 4:45: goal and why, baseline, journey and strategies, measurable results and reflection; context (20) rewards confident, specific storytelling backed by healthy living knowledge.','Incorporate the portfolio actively (20)—tabs, charts and before-and-after data; report specific, safe progress even if the goal was not fully reached; deliver with clear voice, eye contact and professionalism.'],terms:[['Context','The verbal explanation of your journey toward the goal.'],['Healthy proof of progress','Evidence that change was achieved safely and benefited health.'],['Visual aid','The portfolio used to show evidence during the presentation.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','Time.'),q('What may be shown to judges?','Only the portfolio','Slides','Props','A video','Rule.'),q('How many points is context worth?','20','5','10','30','Rubric.'),q('How many points is incorporation of the portfolio worth?','20','5','10','15','Rubric.'),q('What happens to the portfolio after the presentation?','It is left with judges for scoring','It is thrown away','It is mailed home','Nothing','Rule.'),q('Which results statement is strongest?','Specific numbers from logs over time','“It went well.”','“I tried.”','“Mostly done.”','Evidence.'),q('If you did not fully reach the goal, you should:','Show progress and explain your plan','Pretend you did','Skip results','Change the goal','Honesty.'),q('When is the time card shown?','With one minute remaining','At the start','At three minutes','Never','Timing.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline your five-minute Healthy Living presentation, explaining how you would use your portfolio as evidence.',rubric:[['Goal and baseline',['smart goal','why','baseline','starting','measurements']],['Journey and context',['strategies','setback','adjusted','confident','knowledge','motivated']],['Portfolio and results',['portfolio','charts','logs','tabs','point','measurable','results']],['Structure and delivery',['4:45','reflection','eye contact','voice','one minute','professional']]],model:'I would open my portfolio to the title page and state my SMART goal: to run one mile in under nine minutes by May 1 by running four days a week and tracking each run in a fitness log, explaining that I chose it because my baseline assessment showed low cardiorespiratory fitness and I wanted more energy for school and sports. In about 45 seconds, I would turn to my baseline assessment and point out my starting mile time of 12 minutes and my resting heart rate. For the next two minutes, I would describe my journey using my logs: starting with run-walk intervals, applying progressive overload by adding about 10% each week, using the talk test and heart rate to manage intensity, getting eight to nine hours of sleep and adjusting after a two-week illness by restarting at a lower distance. I would hold up my progress graph to show results—my mile time improving from 12:00 to 8:55 over 16 weeks—and explain healthy proof of progress, such as a lower resting heart rate and better afternoon energy, along with a letter from my coach. I would close with a short reflection on what I learned about behavior change and my plan to keep running three days a week. I would rehearse to about 4 minutes 45 seconds, organize portfolio tabs to match my order, hold charts toward the judges, make eye contact, speak confidently and leave the portfolio with judges afterward.'},sources:[RULES,APA]};
